    "heroImageDescription": "A photograph of Moss and Hawthorn together, ideally side by side in the 1958 paddock or on a podium, capturing friendly rivalry; alternatively Hawthorn's Ferrari Dino 246 on the Boavista street circuit, 24 August 1958 (Bernard Cahier, Getty Images). Licensing: Getty Images / Motorsport Images editorial licence required.",
    "story": "Let's start with an uncomfortable fact: Stirling Moss, the driver many people still call the greatest never to win a world championship, lost his best chance of doing so because he was too decent to let his rival be disqualified.\n\nToday that sounds like a fairy tale. In 1958 it was just Stirling.\n\nThat season was a duel between two Englishmen with very different machinery and very different personalities. Moss drove the green Vanwall, a British car he had championed. Mike Hawthorn drove for Ferrari: tall, charismatic, a bow tie on race day, as quick as anyone when the mood took him. Moss won races; Hawthorn collected points. By late summer it was close.\n\nAt the Portuguese Grand Prix, held on the cobbled streets of Boavista in Porto, Moss dominated. Hawthorn, battling for second, spun late in the race and stalled. To get going again he pushed the car in the wrong direction, against the flow of the circuit, before bump-starting it and rejoining to finish second.\n\nThe stewards were unimpressed. Moving against the direction of the race was, they believed, grounds for disqualification. If Hawthorn lost those points, Moss's path to the title would become dramatically easier.\n\nSo what did Moss do? He walked into the stewards' room and argued for Hawthorn. He explained that his rival had not been on the racing circuit when he pushed the car, and so had broken no rule. The stewards accepted it. Hawthorn kept his points.\n\nFast forward to the final round in Morocco. Moss did everything he could, winning the race and setting fastest lap, which was worth a point back then. It wasn't enough. Hawthorn finished second and took the championship by a single point, even though he had won just one Grand Prix all year to Moss's four.\n\nThink about that arithmetic for a moment. The exact margin Moss lost by was smaller than the points he had personally defended in Portugal.\n\nMoss was asked about it for the rest of his life, and he never expressed regret. To him, there was no decision to make. Winning a title because a rival had been wrongly punished would not have felt like winning at all.\n\nThere is a bittersweet ending, too. Hawthorn, Britain's first world champion, retired immediately after the title. His teammate and close friend Peter Collins had been killed at the Nürburgring that August, and the joy had drained out of the sport for him. Within months, in January 1959, Hawthorn himself died in a road accident near Guildford. He never had the chance to grow old as champion.\n\nMoss went on to win many more races, to drive some of the most astonishing races of his era, and to become the face of British motorsport for decades. But no crown ever came.\n\nWe often say Formula 1 is a ruthless sport, and mostly it is. That's exactly why 1958 matters. It reminds us that the sport's most famous champion-who-wasn't chose integrity over a trophy, and never once pretended it had been a sacrifice.",
