    "heroImageDescription": "A period portrait of Maria Teresa de Filippis seated in her Maserati 250F at Spa in 1958, helmet on or held in her lap; companion image of Lella Lombardi in the March 751 at Montjuïc, 27 April 1975 (Getty Images / Studio Colombo). Licensing: Getty Images, Motorsport Images or Rex/Shutterstock editorial licences required; no CC-licensed equivalent assumed.",
    "story": "How many young girls watching Formula 1 today know the name Maria Teresa de Filippis? Probably very few. And that, frankly, is a failure of how we tell this sport's history.\n\nDe Filippis was born in Naples in 1926. By the 1950s she was racing seriously in Italy, and in 1958, with help from the Ferrari driver Luigi Musso, she stepped up to Formula 1 in a privately entered Maserati 250F, the same model that had carried Fangio to his final title the year before. Her first outing, in a non-championship race at Syracuse, brought a fifth place.\n\nThat May she entered the Monaco Grand Prix. She didn't qualify, which at Monaco in those days was nothing unusual; only the fastest made the cut on that tight little street circuit. A few weeks later, at the vast, frightening Spa-Francorchamps, she started a World Championship Grand Prix. She was the first woman ever to do so. She drove a steady race and finished tenth, a couple of laps down on the winner, on a circuit that terrified seasoned professionals.\n\nShe entered a handful more races, and later recalled facing hostility from officials who simply didn't think a woman belonged. But what ended her career wasn't prejudice. It was grief. When her friend and mentor Jean Behra was killed in 1959, she stepped away from racing altogether. She lived until 2016, when she died aged 89, still Formula 1's first lady.\n\nFor years after she left, no woman started a Grand Prix. Then came Lella Lombardi.\n\nLombardi was a different kind of pioneer: a driver who had worked her way up through the junior categories, racing hard and winning respect from the people who mattered. She reached Formula 1 in the mid-1970s, and her place in the record books was written at the 1975 Spanish Grand Prix on the Montjuïc street circuit in Barcelona.\n\nIt was an awful weekend. Drivers protested about the barriers before the race even began, and during it Rolf Stommelen's car crashed into the crowd, killing spectators. The race was stopped well short of distance. Lombardi was running sixth at the time, and because less than three-quarters of the race had been completed, the points were halved.\n\nHalf a point. That's the total haul of every woman who has ever raced in the World Championship. It's a statistic that gets thrown around as trivia, but sit with it for a moment. Half a century has passed, and Lella Lombardi remains the only woman on the scoreboard.\n\nHere's my view: neither of these women should be remembered as a novelty. De Filippis started a Grand Prix at Spa in a car that demanded courage just to sit in. Lombardi raced in an era when cars and circuits could kill without warning, and she did it on merit.\n\nTheir story matters because it is unfinished. Every time the paddock debates why there are no women on the grid, the answer starts here, with two Italians who went first and found the door only halfway open.",
    "whyItMatters": "Two Italian women proved they belonged on the grid long before the sport was ready to admit it, and their story is a reminder of how much talent F1 has never given a chance.",
