    "id": 13,
    "storyNumber": "13",
    "slug": "adelaide-1986-mansell-tyre",
    "title": "The Tyre That Exploded a Championship",
    "subtitle": "Nigel Mansell was minutes from the title in Adelaide. Then, at full speed, his left-rear tyre disintegrated — and Alain Prost was champion.",
    "category": "Championship",
    "year": 1986,
    "era": "1980s",
    "grandPrix": "1986 Australian Grand Prix, Adelaide",
    "peopleInvolved": [
      "Nigel Mansell",
      "Nelson Piquet",
      "Alain Prost",
      "Keke Rosberg",
      "Williams",
      "McLaren"
    ],
    "heroImageDescription": "The iconic image of Nigel Mansell's Williams FW11 with its left-rear tyre shredding and sparks flying down the Brabham Straight in Adelaide, 26 October 1986 (Motorsport Images / DPPI, as used by Motor Sport). Licensing: Motorsport Images / DPPI editorial licence.",
    "story": "There are moments in Formula 1 that every fan of a certain age can see with their eyes closed. One of them is a red, white and yellow Williams hurtling down a straight in Adelaide, sparks pouring from the rear, its tyre torn to pieces, and Nigel Mansell fighting to keep the car out of the wall.\n\nIt's the image of a championship disappearing in a fraction of a second.\n\nThe 1986 season had been an extraordinary three-way fight. The Williams-Honda was the fastest car on the grid, and it had two drivers who could not stand each other. Nelson Piquet, already a double world champion, had expected to be the team's undisputed number one. Nigel Mansell, the gritty, emotional Englishman, refused to play that role. The two took points from each other all year, and their rivalry became one of the sport's great soap operas.\n\nThat gave an opening to Alain Prost. His McLaren was slower, but Prost was the master of consistency, collecting points whenever the Williams pair stumbled.\n\nArriving in Adelaide for the final round, Mansell led with 70 points, Prost had 64 and Piquet 63. Mansell only needed to finish third to be champion, even if one of his rivals won. On paper, it was his title to lose.\n\nThe race began well enough. Mansell was running comfortably in a championship-winning position. Then, at about two-thirds distance, the first warning came. Keke Rosberg, in his final Grand Prix, retired from the lead when a rear tyre failed on his McLaren.\n\nAt the time, many thought it was an isolated problem. A few laps later, Mansell was flying down the long Brabham Straight at enormous speed when his left-rear tyre exploded. The car slewed and sparked. By some combination of skill and luck, he wrestled it to a stop in the escape road without hitting anything. Then he climbed out, and the cameras caught the look on his face, a man who knew it was over.\n\nWilliams immediately called Piquet into the pits for fresh tyres as a precaution, which dropped him behind Prost. Piquet chased hard all the way to the flag, but Prost held on to win the race and the championship.\n\nIt was a staggering turnaround. Prost became the first driver to win back-to-back titles since Jack Brabham in 1959 and 1960, and he did it in a car that, in pure pace terms, should not have been in the fight at all.\n\nThe race is often voted among the greatest in history, and Motor Sport magazine once ranked it the very best. You can see why. It had everything: a fierce team rivalry, a cool-headed outsider, technical failure and an ending no one could have predicted an hour earlier.\n\nWhat I love about Adelaide 1986 is that everybody comes out of it with their reputations intact. Prost proved his genius for staying in contention. Piquet showed his speed. And Mansell, who lost everything that afternoon, became even more of a hero to his fans because of how cruel it was. He would finally get his championship in 1992, and when he did, you can bet plenty of people were thinking about a straight in Adelaide.",
    "whyItMatters": "Adelaide 1986 is still the ultimate reminder that in F1, a championship isn't won until the very last lap — and sometimes not even then.",
